== Republican primary ==

=== Candidates ===
* [[Fred Tuttle]], dairy farmer and actor
* [[John A. McMullen|Jack McMullen]], businessman

=== Campaign ===
McMullen had maintained a vacation home in [[Warren, Vermont]] beginning in 1983, and he made Vermont his permanent residence beginning in 1997.{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}

In 1998, he declared his candidacy for the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nomination for U.S. Senator.{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  As related by Chris Graff, longtime Vermont bureau chief for the [[Associated Press]], McMullen's candidacy sustained an immediate blow when Graff interviewed retired Senator [[Robert Stafford]] about the [[January 1998 North American ice storm|January 1998 ice storm]] and other current events.{{sfn|He was in right place, at right time in history}}  During the discussion, Stafford persistently got McMullen's name wrong, calling him "Mulholland".{{sfn|He was in right place, at right time in history}}  Graff wrote that he tried to politely correct Stafford, but finally realized that Stafford's intent was to convey his opinion that McMullen was too unknown and too new to Vermont to be a viable candidate.{{sfn|He was in right place, at right time in history}}  The [[Lead paragraph|lede]] in the resulting story was that Vermont's senior Republican was of the view that McMullen had not lived in the state long enough to represent it in the senate, and Stafford's dismissal of McMullen as "Mulholland or whatever his name is" became a [[Running gag|running joke]] among reporters and political operatives.{{sfn|He was in right place, at right time in history}}

In the Republican primary, McMullen faced [[Fred Tuttle]], a retired dairy farmer who had starred in a mock documentary film called ''[[Man with a Plan (film)|Man with a Plan]]'', a comedy about a retired farmer who decides to run for Vermont's seat in the [[United States House of Representatives]].{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  Tuttle and filmmaker [[John O'Brien (filmmaker)|John O'Brien]] considered using a Tuttle candidacy to promote the film, and accepted a suggestion from political columnist Peter Freyne to challenge McMullen rather than incumbent Congressman [[Bernie Sanders]].{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}  Tuttle then ran in the Republican U.S. Senate primary, partly to generate awareness of the movie, and partly to mock McMullen as a [[carpetbagger]] and flatlander (Vermont slang for an out-of-stater) who had moved to Vermont only because he thought it would be easier to run for the Senate there than in more populous [[Massachusetts]].{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}  McMullen was slow to recognize that the Tuttle/O'Brien strategy was viable; when the McMullen campaign challenged Tuttle's nominating petitions as 23 short of the required 500 signatures, Tuttle turned in an additional 2,300.{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}

During a debate before the primary, Tuttle underscored McMullen's newness to Vermont.{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}  One question he posed to McMullen was "What's a [[Tedder (machine)|tedder]]?"{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}} (A machine for drying hay.){{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  Another was "What's rowen?" (The second cutting of [[hay]] from the same field in one season.){{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  Tuttle continued with queries like "How many [[teat]]s a [[Holstein Friesian cattle|Holstein]] got?"{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  (Four; McMullen guessed six.){{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  Tuttle then provided McMullen a list of local place names and asked him to pronounce them.{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}  McMullen's inexperience with Vermont geography showed, as he mispronounced names including [[Leicester, Vermont|Leicester]] (Lester is correct), [[Calais, Vermont|Calais]] (callous being the correct way), and [[Charlotte, Vermont|Charlotte]] (shar-LOT, not Shar-Lit).{{sfn|"Reflecting On The 1998 Tuttle-McMullen Debate"}}

The campaign made national headlines, and added to Tuttle's semi-celebrity status.{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}  Despite McMullen spending over $500,000 to Tuttle's $200, on primary day, Tuttle beat McMullen 28,355 votes to 23,321 (54% to 44%).{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}  Tuttle immediately announced his intention to vote for incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] Senator [[Patrick Leahy]], after which the two made several joint appearances.{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}  On election day, Leahy defeated Tuttle and several minor candidates to win reelection.{{sfn|"Baruth: The Political Art Behind Fred Tuttle, The Man With A Plan"}}

